The provided output appears to be a list of buttons on an Android screen. The `resource_id` field is empty for most buttons, indicating that they do not have any associated resources.

To extract the actual button text from this list, we can iterate through the list and extract the `text` field for each button:

```java
List<Button> buttons = new ArrayList<>();
// populate the list with Button objects

for (Button button : buttons) {
    if (!button.getText().toString().isEmpty()) {
        System.out.println(button.getText());
    }
}
```

This code will print out the text of all non-empty buttons in the list.

Alternatively, we can use a more concise approach using Java 8 streams:

```java
buttons.stream()
       .filter(button -> !button.getText().toString().isEmpty())
       .map(Button::getText)
       .forEach(System.out::println);
```

This code achieves the same result as the previous example but in a more compact form.
